    "narrative": "The Austin Waves head into today\u2019s slate with **Gunnar Henderson** and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** anchoring our production, both sporting scores above 81. **Gunnar Henderson** represents our premier play; he benefits from a highly favorable matchup against Nick Martinez (xERA 4.70), and the combination of park factors and the #3 spot in the order elevates his daily efficiency significantly. Similarly, **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** is a must-start against Carlos Rod\u00f3n. Despite Rod\u00f3n\u2019s pedigree, the 4.30 xERA suggests vulnerability, and our model is banking on **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.**\u2019s elite BvP history (26 PA) and a strong platoon edge to produce a high-ceiling performance. **Jackson Merrill** also warrants special attention; while his raw projection is solid, the matchup against Bailey Ober is optimized by a slow-delivery variable that boosts his stolen base potential to 92%, a critical factor in our strategy given the scarcity of speed.\n\nElsewhere in the lineup, we are capitalizing on several key platoon and environmental advantages. **Luis Robert Jr.** and **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** are both set for productive afternoons, with **Luis Robert Jr.** buoyed by a beneficial platoon split against Sean Newcomb and favorable wind conditions, and **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** enjoying a 14% boost from a favorable platoon matchup against Javy Soriano. The most difficult decision this morning was evaluating **Miguel Vargas** and **Coby Mayo**. While **Miguel Vargas** holds a sub-50 score, his elite xwOBA (0.387) and wind-aided park factor kept him in the lineup over bench options like **Heliot Ramos**, who remains unavailable today. **Coby Mayo** earns the utility spot largely due to the same high-run-environment park factors that benefit **Gunnar Henderson**, successfully tipping the scale despite a slight drag from his platoon split.\n\nWe are monitoring the health of our depth, as **Andy Pages**, **Jordan Westburg**, and **Jacob Wilson** remain sidelined on the IL, limiting our late-game maneuverability. While we are comfortable with our starters, we remain wary of **Liam Hicks**, whose 57.76 score is respectable but sits on a razor\u2019s edge due to Shane Baz\u2019s 4.22 xERA; he is a candidate for a pinch-hit scenario if the game flow dictates. With **Josh Jung**, **Dominic Smith**, **Curtis Mead**, **Wade Meckler**, **Luis Lara**, and **Tommy White** also currently outside the immediate rotation, the focus remains on extracting maximum efficiency from our core, specifically leaning into the high-upside speed threats that define our roster\u2019s identity.",
